+# Flash Next at --tp 2: the indexer keys without a whole copy on each rank
+
+Built on the two-rank MXFP8 head cuts and NVFP4 KV (on upstream PR #1784's head split).
+Their per-stream bytes at a 262,160-token window, NVFP4 KV at half heads: rank 0 2.225 GiB, rank 1 1.999 GiB, of
+which the indexer keys are 0.938 GiB on each rank. The bar is at least 28 streams with a usable prompt cache.
+
+## What the indexer holds and who reads it (checked against the code)
+
+Each of the 12 attention layers keeps two indexer caches per stream (`cuda_seq_bytes.zig`):
+
+| Cache | Row | Rows | At 262,160 tokens, 12 layers |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Raw keys `ikc` | 128 bf16 (256 B) | one per position | 0.750 GiB |
+| Pooled blocks `pooled` | 128 bf16 (256 B) | one per 4 positions | 0.188 GiB |
+
+Readers, from every launch site of the two buffers:
+
+- **Raw keys are read only by pooling.** `fn_qsa_pool_body` (`flashnext_attn.cu`; plain, media, paged and lanes entry
+  points) reads the 4 raw keys of each block that the current rows complete. Prep writes them; nothing else reads them.
+  When a pass starts at position `p0`, every block before `p0 / 4` is already pooled, so the only raw keys a later pass
+  can read are positions `4 * floor(p0 / 4)` to `p0 - 1` (at most 3 rows), plus the rows the pass writes itself.
+- **Pooled blocks are read only by the scores kernel** (`fn_qsa_scores`, `qsa_tile::scores_body`), and by snapshots.
+- **Scores are per block.** A block's score is `sum over 4 heads of relu(q_h . k_b) / sqrt(128)` in a fixed order. It
+  depends only on the row's indexer query and that block's pooled row, not on which other blocks exist.
+- **Selection** (`fn_qsa_select_body`, `flashnext_batch.cu`) maps each score to a monotone u32 key (`fn_qsa_key`), and
+  runs a 4-pass radix select for the 512th largest key (`cut`). It takes every key above `cut`, then the first `need`
+  keys equal to `cut` in ascending block order, and writes the chosen blocks in ascending block order, followed by the
+  incomplete tail. So the chosen set is exactly **the top 512 under (key descending, block index ascending)**, and its
+  order is ascending block. No reduction order across blocks is involved.
+- Rows with at most 512 complete blocks (positions below 2,052) attend densely: no scores, no selection.
+- **Rewinds are bounded.** The DeltaNet states are recurrent, so a sequence only rewinds within its verify window
+  (`Ops.rollback`: `fed = fed0 + keep`), to 0 (`cuda_mtp_check`), or to a snapshot's position (restore).
+- At two ranks every pass goes through `Pass.attnRows` lane by lane (`batch_lanes.useAttn` refuses half heads), on
+  our own glue (the captured set refuses half heads). Paged KV, decode graphs and `--slide` are refused at `--tp 2`.
+
+## Design B, built: a window of raw keys (no exchange, exact by construction)
+
+Since only the current block's raw keys outlive a pass, each stream keeps a window of `W` raw-key rows per layer
+instead of `cap` rows. A position's row sits at `position - base`, where `base` (a multiple of 4, per layer) only moves
+forward.
+
+- **Before a layer's prep** (`Pass.indexKeys`, called by `attnRows`), for rows `p0 .. p0 + n - 1`:
+  - `first = 4 * floor(p0 / 4)` is the first raw key the pooling can read.
+  - If `p0 + n - base > W`, the window moves: rows `first .. p0 - 1` (at most 3) are copied to rows `0 .. p0 - first - 1`
+    with one `cuMemcpyDtoDAsync` on the pass's stream, and `base = first`. A move needs `n + 8 <= W`, else the pass is
+    refused (`error.IndexWindowShort`); with that margin the copy's source and destination never overlap.
+  - If `first < base` (only after a reset to 0, which needs no rows), `base = first` when `p0 == first`; any other case
+    is refused (`error.IndexWindowBehind`).
+- **The kernels are unchanged.** Prep, the media prep, the quantized preps and pooling all address `IKC + pos * 128`,
+  so the pass hands them `ikc.ptr - base * 256`: the same device addresses as `position - base`. Every row they write
+  or read lies in `[base, base + W)`. The arithmetic is untouched, so pooled rows, scores, selection and replies are
+  the bytes they were: no new exactness argument is needed beyond "the same values reach the same kernels".
+- **Window size:** `W = alignForward(max(chunk, 512) + 8, 64)`, capped at `cap`. A stream's rows in one pass are at
+  most the engine's chunk (forward, windows) or 512 (the MTP head's `2 * piece_rows`, which can grow the shared
+  scratch), and a round adds 1 a stream. At the default chunk of 1,536, `W` is 1,600 rows: 4.7 MiB a stream for all 12
+  layers, against 768 MiB at 262,160 tokens. A window of `cap` rows (a short stream) never moves, so it is the old
+  layout.
+- **Snapshots** carry each rank's raw-key carry: the `at % 4` rows from `4 * floor(at / 4)`, read at `row - base`.
+  Restore writes them to rows 0.. and sets `base = 4 * floor(at / 4)`. `cuda_snap.bytes*` / `order*` take the layout,
+  so a kept state's size still comes from the cache's own byte functions, and the snapshot design's rank-1 inequality (docs/flashnext-tp2-snapshots.md) is unchanged
+  (both ranks hold the same carry). A state from one layout is refused by a sequence of the other before any copy (`restore`; the snapshot design moves
+  its refusals into `fits`).
+- **Scope:** on only at the head split (`--tp 2`), on both ranks. `TF_FLASHNEXT_INDEX_WINDOW=0` keeps whole raw keys
+  (the rollback). Both ranks must agree: the setting is in the rank `Identity`. TP=1 is unchanged (no window, the same
+  code path). The MTP head's own cache (rank 0, whole layer) keeps whole raw keys for now: the same change there saves
+  another 64 MiB a stream on rank 0 (1.75 GiB at 28 streams); listed under follow-ups.
+- **Cost:** no collective, no new kernel. One 3-row copy a layer when the window moves: once a prompt chunk, about
+  once every 1,590 decode positions.
+
+Per-stream indexer bytes per rank at 262,160 tokens: **0.188 GiB pooled + 0.005 GiB window, instead of 0.938 GiB**.
+That is 0.745 GiB a stream saved on each rank (800,440,320 bytes), more than the position split's 0.469 GiB, and with
+no exchange.
+
+## Design A, specified, not built: split the pooled blocks by position
+
+The first direction considered, checked and found exact. With B in place it saves another 0.094 GiB a stream a rank
+(2.6 GiB at 28 streams) for one collective a layer a pass and four kernel changes. Build it if the tables below fall
+short of the bar after B.
+
+**Layout.** Groups of `G = 64` blocks (256 positions) alternate between the ranks: block `b` belongs to rank
+`(b / 64) % 2`, at local row `(b / 128) * 64 + b % 64`. 64 is the scores kernel's tile, so a tile is one group: its
+loads stay contiguous. A rank holds `localCount(blocks, rank)` pooled rows (host: `cuda_index_split.zig`). Local order
+is increasing in global order. The raw-key window (B) stays whole on both ranks (it is tiny); each rank pools only its
+own blocks. Without B, raw keys would split by the same owner rule (position `q` goes with block `q / 4`).
+
+**Selection, a row with `complete > 512` blocks:**
+1. Each rank scores its own complete blocks (same per-block arithmetic, local addresses).
+2. Each rank selects its local top `min(512, local)` under (key desc, block asc) with the same radix select. Because
+   local order follows global order, "first equal keys in local order" is "first in global order".
+3. One `ncclAllGather` of the candidate rows: 512 entries of (u32 key, i32 global block), 4 KiB a row a rank. Both
+   ranks know each other's candidate count from the position alone, so no count is sent.
+4. Each rank merges the two block-ascending lists into one block-ascending list (at most 1,024), runs the same radix
+   select on it (keys, ties to the lower block), and writes `ids` in ascending block order, then the tail.
+
+**Exactness.** Let `S` be one GPU's choice: the 512 greatest (key, block) pairs under the total order above. An element
+of `S` held by rank `r` is among the 512 greatest of rank `r`'s pairs (a subset's top contains every global-top member
+it holds), so `S` is a subset of the union of candidates. The 512 greatest of any superset of `S` within the full set
+are `S`. The keys are the same bits on both sides (per-block scores, same pooled rows), ties break on the global block
+index in both, and the output order is ascending block in both. So `ids`, `nkr` and `spr` equal one GPU's, and
+attention (unchanged, on each rank's own KV head shard, which holds every position) gives one GPU's bytes.
+The host reference (`cuda_index_split.zig`) ports `fn_qsa_select_body` and tests merge == one GPU on random scores
+with heavy ties.
+
+**Cost.** One all-gather a full-attention layer a pass: 12 a pass, against 192 a pass today (four a layer:
+`split_lane.out`'s two gathers, the shared expert's gather, the expert sum). At two ranks each verify window is its own
+pass, so this is +6% collectives a pass. Bytes: `rows * 4 KiB` a rank a layer: a 7-row decode window 28 KiB, a 1,536-row
+prompt chunk 6 MiB (72 MiB a chunk over 12 layers, about 3 ms at 200 Gb/s, against seconds of compute).
+
+**Device work it needs (sm_121):** new entry points (the old ones are shared with qwen4, `qwen4/cuda_attention.zig`):
+pool only owned blocks at local rows; scores over local tiles writing local columns; a candidate-emitting select; a
+merge-select over the gathered candidates; contract records for each. Plus the all-gather in `attnRows` between
+select and attention, and snapshot pieces for the local pooled rows.
+
+**Prefill and MTP.** Prompt rows select per 256-row block; the gather is per block (`att_rows * 4 KiB` = 1 MiB). The
+fused attention launch is unchanged (it reads `ids`). The MTP head's layer runs on rank 0 only, whole, and would not
+split.
+
+## Memory per rank (NVFP4 KV, half heads, MTP on, chunk 1,536)
+
+Per-stream charges at the whole-key layout's 262,160-token window, from `cuda_lanes_load.streamBytesIn` (rank 0 with the MTP head's
+cache, rank 1 its verify window). `cuda_index_window_test.zig` pins the bytes; GiB here are those divided by 2^30.
+A sequence itself caps at 262,144 tokens (`prompt.max_tokens`); the 16 extra rows change nothing visible here.
+
+| Per stream | Rank 0 | Rank 1 |
+|---|---|---|
+| Whole raw keys (2,389,335,502 / 2,145,891,842 B) | 2.225 GiB | 1.999 GiB |
+| **B, the raw-key window, built** (1,588,895,182 / 1,345,451,522 B) | **1.480 GiB** | **1.253 GiB** |
+| B + the MTP head's window (follow-up, not built) | 1.418 GiB | 1.253 GiB |
+| B + A, the pooled split (not built) | 1.386 GiB | 1.159 GiB |
+
+All streams, GiB, and what is left of rank 0's ~66 GiB of room (the whole-key layout's figure) for the prompt cache:
+
+| Streams | Whole keys rank 0 | left | Whole keys rank 1 | **B rank 0** | **left** | B rank 1 | B+A rank 0 | left | B+A rank 1 |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| 16 | 35.6 | 30.4 | 32.0 | 23.7 | 42.3 | 20.0 | 22.2 | 43.8 | 18.5 |
+| 24 | 53.4 | 12.6 | 48.0 | 35.5 | 30.5 | 30.1 | 33.3 | 32.7 | 27.8 |
+| 28 | 62.3 | 3.7 | 56.0 | 41.4 | **24.6** | 35.1 | 38.8 | 27.2 | 32.5 |
+| 32 | 71.2 | -5.2 | 64.0 | 47.4 | 18.6 | 40.1 | 44.4 | 21.6 | 37.1 |
+
+So 28 streams leave about 24.6 GiB on rank 0 instead of 3.7, and 32 streams fit with 18.6 GiB to spare. Rank 1
+needs less than rank 0 in every row, and the startup room is the smaller of the two ranks' rooms.
+
+Kept prompt states shrink too. Their raw keys were 256 bytes a token a layer (3 KiB a token over 12 layers, more than
+the NVFP4 KV itself at half heads, 3.6 KiB). They are now at most 3 rows a layer, so a state at 262,143 tokens is
+768 MiB smaller (805,294,080 bytes, pinned in the test).
+
+## Needs an sm_121 build or a GPU (none of it runs here)
+
+- The engine build for sm_121 and a two-rank start with `--kv-cache nvfp4`, drafts and the prompt cache on.
+- Two-rank replies with the window equal one GPU's: cold prompts past 2,052 tokens (selection active), past one
+  window move (longer than `W`), a prompt longer than the chunk, drafted equals `draft:false`, and resumed from a
+  snapshot equals fresh (a snapshot at a position that is not a multiple of 4).
+- `TF_FLASHNEXT_INDEX_WINDOW=0` at two ranks equals the window's replies, and TP=1 replies are unchanged.
+- Memory: each rank's allocation after load and at full streams matches the ledger.
+- An image prompt at two ranks (the media prep and pooling take the shifted pointer too).
+- Design A, if built: every new kernel, its contract records and launch limits, and the all-gather.
