@@ -77,8 +81,32 @@ tensorfold-native serve /path/to/checkpoint --temperature 0
 
 - `--context N` takes up to the model's 262,144 tokens; the default is the whole window. Past 2,048 keys each attention
   layer reads 512 blocks of 4 keys a row, chosen by the model's block indexer, as the Python engine does.
+- `TF_FLASHNEXT_YARN=4` (one rank) serves `--context` past the trained window, up to 4 x 262,144: Qwen's YaRN
+  recipe (rope_type yarn, factor 4, the ramp over pairs 14 to 22), with the attention factor 0.1 ln 4 + 1 folded into
+  the rotated norms' gammas as the Python engine folds it. `TF_FLASHNEXT_YARN_RAMP_POSITIONS` moves the ramp (vLLM's
+  M-RoPE: 4 x 262,144). Unset, 0 or off: the default rope, bit for bit.
 - `--parallel N` admits N streams. Each stream's caches are its own: about 110 MiB of DeltaNet state and the n-gram
   tail, plus 28 KiB a token of keys, values and indexer keys, and with drafts its MTP head's cache.
+- `TF_FLASHNEXT_SHARED_POOL=1` (one rank) charges each stream only its fixed part at startup (about 0.32 GiB with
+  the MTP head and vision), so the prompt cache gets its `--prompt-cache-gib` beside them; a stream's caches are
+  admitted when it opens (its prompt + max_tokens), kept prompt states giving theirs back (never the one it restores),
+  else it waits for a stream to end. `TENSORFOLD_MEMORY_FLOOR_GIB` (default the reserve up to 10 GiB) frees kept states
+  while a new stream would leave MemAvailable under it. Unset: every stream is charged its full window, as before.
+- `--kv-cache fp8` (the engine's own glue, contiguous caches; not with `--slide`, `TF_FLASHNEXT_KV_PAGES`,
+  `--learn-conversations` or `--spill-gib`) keeps keys and values as e4m3 codes with a
+  power-of-two scale a position and KV head: 1,056 bytes a position and layer instead of 2,048, so a token's caches take
+  about 17.5 KiB instead of 30 KiB (12 layers and the MTP head's). The indexer's keys, the pooled blocks and the DeltaNet
+  states stay bf16. Attention reads the codes widened to bf16 exactly, so it is the bf16 kernels' arithmetic on rows
+  rounded to e4m3: lossy against a bf16 cache, deterministic for a given prompt. bf16 stays the default.
+- `--kv-cache nvfp4` and `--kv-cache fp8k_nvfp4v` (same limits as fp8) keep values, and with nvfp4 keys too, as NVFP4:
+  a row of 128 code bytes (two E2M1 nibbles each) and 16 E4M3 block scales under a power-of-two row scale, 608 and 832
+  bytes a position and layer, so a token's caches take about 11.8 and 14.6 KiB (12,064 and 14,976 bytes with the
+  indexer's keys). The format is an earlier Python-engine NVFP4 cache's, byte for byte (cuda_kv.zig); reads widen it
+  to bf16 exactly, as fp8's do. The MTP head's own cache stays bf16 while the stream fits its charge
+  (`TF_FLASHNEXT_HEAD_KV=main` keeps it in the main format).
+- The three quantized formats serve at `--tp 2` too: each rank keeps its own KV head's rows of every attention layer
+  (half a GPU's keys and values; the indexer's keys stay whole on both), with the same kernels. Rank 0 keeps the MTP
+  head's whole cache; rank 1's verify windows keep none. Both ranks must name the same `--kv-cache`.
 - `--no-drafts` serves without the MTP head; `"draft": false` on a request does the same for that request.
 - `TF_FLASHNEXT_READOUT_ROWS` (default 64, `0` never) is the rows from which the hyper-connection read-out takes the prompt
   tiles. Same bits either way; `128` keeps 16-stream decode rounds on the row kernels, up to 7% quicker on a GB10.
